{"text":"Recent work supports modeling directed associations separately from semantic similarity. [TransGAT (ICDM 2024)](https://www.amazon.science/publications/transitivity-encoded-graph-attention-networks-for-complementary-item-recommendations) encodes behavioral direction and couples complementary/substitute graphs, addressing noisy, sparse co-purchases. [Revisiting Graph Projections (2025)](https://arxiv.org/html/2506.09209v1) offers a lightweight alternative: project user–item interactions into weighted item links, then adjust direction using inverse-distance-weighted purchase order within short windows. Important limitation: its sparsity treatment uses item-category transitions, unavailable here; reported improvements do not establish gains under the pinned RecHarness protocol.

Content–sequence hybrids provide architectural inspiration, but pretrained semantics often supply their advantage. [SAGERec (2025)](https://www.mdpi.com/2079-9292/14/24/4844) combines a sparse semantic graph, adaptive edge weighting and a Transformer, explicitly relying on LLM embeddings. [AlleCompanion (2026)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.05063) filters co-purchase noise through category constraints, expert feedback and LLM reasoning. Neither transfers directly under the available-data and no-pretrained-weights constraints.

A plausible experiment—not an established result—is SASRec plus a sparse directed transition-score residual, with separate source/target embeddings and a small title/description encoder trained from scratch. Use lexical features or pooled trainable token embeddings to share information across sparse items; tune graph/content contributions through macro validation HR@10 and ablate each branch. Interpret learned edges as predictive associations: temporal order and textual resemblance do not establish functional complementarity, substitution or exact compatibility. Avoid assigning those labels without supporting observations."}
